          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Maintenance and Whole-House Options</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          An under sink filter is the step up from a pitcher that most kitchens actually need: filtered water on tap, no refilling, and cartridges that last months instead of weeks. The hard part is not finding one, it is reading the certifications well enough to know which of them reduces the contaminant you care about, and whether you need reverse osmosis at all.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not lab-test these systems. We built the shortlist the way a careful buyer should: by reading the certification each maker lists, the performance data sheet where one exists, and the capacity and flow ratings, then cross-checking them against retailer listings and certification trackers. Where a retailer and the manufacturer disagree, we report both and tell you which to trust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification was the first filter. A system that claims to remove lead without being certified to NSF/ANSI 53 by an accredited body is asking you to take its word for it, and there are hundreds of those listings. Every pick here lists a certification from NSF, WQA or IAPMO R&T, the three bodies most commonly accredited to certify drinking water treatment units in the United States.
          </p>

          <p>
            Capacity and running cost came next. A cheap system with a 300-gallon cartridge can cost more over three years than an expensive one with an 8,000-gallon cartridge, so we compared rated gallons and replacement intervals, not just the sticker price. Flow rate mattered too, because a filter that takes a minute to fill a pot gets bypassed, and a bypassed filter does nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we looked at installation honestly. Some systems need a hole drilled in your sink or countertop, some need a power outlet under the sink, and some need a drain connection. None of that is a deal-breaker, but it changes which system is right for a rental, a stone countertop or a cramped cabinet. If you are still deciding whether to filter at the sink or for the whole house, our guide to the <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">best whole-house water filtration systems</a> covers the other half of that decision.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Carbon, Reverse Osmosis or Tankless RO: The Three Types
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carbon block filters are the simplest under sink systems. Water passes through one or more cartridges of compressed activated carbon, often with a sediment pre-filter in front. A good carbon block can reduce chlorine taste and odor, and when it is certified to NSF/ANSI 53 it can also reduce lead, cysts and some volatile organic chemicals. Carbon keeps calcium and magnesium in the water and sends nothing to the drain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reverse osmosis pushes water through a semi-permeable membrane that rejects most dissolved solids, including salts, nitrates, fluoride and the minerals that make water hard. RO systems pair the membrane with carbon pre- and post-filters, so they also cover chlorine and organics. The cost is wastewater, a slower process and the removal of minerals some people want to keep, which is why remineralization stages exist.
          </p>

          <p>
            Traditional RO stores filtered water in a pressurized tank under the sink because the membrane produces water slowly. Tankless RO systems use an electric pump to push water through a higher-output membrane fast enough to serve the faucet directly. That frees cabinet space and usually improves the pure-to-drain ratio, but it means plugging the system in.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful rule of thumb: if your water is municipal and your concern is taste, chlorine or lead, a certified carbon system is enough. If you are on well water with high dissolved solids, worried about nitrates or fluoride, or your water report shows contaminants carbon is not certified for, reverse osmosis is the tool. If your issue is a rotten-egg smell, read <a href="/why-does-my-water-smell-like-rotten-eggs/">our guide to sulfur smells in water</a> first, because the fix often belongs at the water heater or the well, not the sink.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 6 Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            We researched these six from manufacturer specifications, performance data sheets and the certifications each maker lists, not from our own lab testing. Every capacity, flow rate and certification below is attributed to the maker or a retailer listing, and where sources disagree we say so. Before you buy, check the exact model number against the NSF, WQA or IAPMO certified-product listings, because certifications are issued per model and lookalike variants are common.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
