        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best under sink water filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most city-water kitchens, the Aquasana Claryum 3-Stage is our pick because Aquasana lists WQA certification to NSF/ANSI 42, 53 and 401 and it keeps minerals without wasting water. If you need reverse osmosis, the Waterdrop G3P800 is the tankless pick, rated at 800 gallons per day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need reverse osmosis or is a carbon filter enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For municipal water where taste, chlorine or lead is the concern, a carbon filter certified to NSF/ANSI 53 is usually enough. Choose reverse osmosis when your water report shows high dissolved solids, nitrates, fluoride or other contaminants that carbon systems are not certified to reduce.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does an under sink filter remove lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is certified for lead reduction, typically under NSF/ANSI 53 or 58. Check the performance data sheet. A filter certified only to NSF/ANSI 42 and 372, like the Waterdrop 10UA, is not certified to reduce lead even though its materials are lead-free.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I change an under sink filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the maker's rating, whichever comes first of gallons or months. Our picks range from 500 gallons or six months for the A.O. Smith AO-US-200 to 8,000 gallons or 12 months for the Waterdrop 10UA, per the manufacturer and retailer listings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install an under sink water filter myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most people can install a carbon system in about an hour with basic tools. Reverse osmosis takes longer because it needs a drain connection and either a tank or a power outlet. Drilling a stone countertop for a dedicated faucet is the job most worth handing to a professional.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does reverse osmosis waste water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The membrane needs a constant flow to flush away rejected contaminants. Older tank systems are commonly described as wasting several gallons per gallon produced, while newer tankless systems advertise better ratios, such as the 3:1 pure-to-drain ratio Waterdrop lists for the G3P800.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an under sink filter better than a pitcher?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is more convenient and usually cheaper per gallon, with no refilling and cartridges that last months. A certified pitcher is a fine choice for renters or small households; our pitcher guide covers the models worth buying. The certification matters more than the format.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

